A||||2||Ferrari_cdn||Durability?||||1||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||11-14-2001||12:23 AM||||I know that lots of cars are usually good for 15-20 years then they are thrown away.  My car is 17 years old, and I'm already in love with it.  I still have the original but rebuilt Duke.  I have plans for a REBUILT 4.9L caddy V8, and I wanted to know if I do this swap, I CAN still have my Fiero for another 10-15 years.  Of course, I realize I might would have to replace struts, shocks, and the likes.  But, generally, I CAN have my Fiero for a LOT longer, or it's like new engine or not, she's gonna go sooner or later.<P>I love Fieros too much already to let them go  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-14-2001||10:13 AM||||If the frame stays in one piece and you replace the components as the age, the car can last a long time.  Rust will be the killer of the frame,  but all other components can be repaired or replaced over time.  The frequency of repairs/replacement depends on the care the car receives.  My 88 has had new front fascia, engine, transmission, struts, paint, sail panels, brakes, tires, battery, head liner, radio, dash panels etc on to infinity on repairs. I don't drive it in the winter, so the rust man won't get it. Like you, I have fun in the car, so I spend the $ to keep it running.||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||11-14-2001||10:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<BR><B>I know that lots of cars are usually good for 15-20 years then they are thrown away.  My car is 17 years old, and I'm already in love with it.  I still have the original but rebuilt Duke.  I have plans for a REBUILT 4.9L caddy V8, and I wanted to know if I do this swap, I CAN still have my Fiero for another 10-15 years.  Of course, I realize I might would have to replace struts, shocks, and the likes.  But, generally, I CAN have my Fiero for a LOT longer, or it's like new engine or not, she's gonna go sooner or later.<P>I love Fieros too much already to let them go   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The answer to your question is yes, if you don't drive it in the winter. If the car is already basically rush free on the underbody, you're in good shape. If you see some spots, typically at the flanges that are rusty, clean, prime, and paint. It's basically all two side galvanized steel and will last a very long long time.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
